WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.880
With 27 features since 2008, the Marvel&nbsp;
Cinematic Universe has given fans some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.480
truly superlative filmgoing experiences.&nbsp;
But which movies are the absolute best?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.920
Which are better off left unwatched in your next&nbsp;
marathon? Keep watching for a complete ranking.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.920
In theory, 2013's The Dark World has everything&nbsp;
you could want. In practice, though, it's just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.080
very fun. Chris Hemsworth is as charming as ever,&nbsp;
and it’s based in part on one of the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.080
comic book runs of all time. It’s got a Star&nbsp;
Wars-style attack on Asgard by spaceships flown by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:44.800
dark elves! It has the rock monsters from Thor's&nbsp;
very first comics appearance! Thor and Loki team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.680
up for revenge against the villain that killed&nbsp;
their mom, climaxing in a fistfight in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.840
Thor and Malekith are literally punching each&nbsp;
other so hard that they land in other dimensions!

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.960
Unfortunately, while all of&nbsp;
that sounds good on paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.880
it's actually both super&nbsp;
boring and generally confusing.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.560
When Nick Fury showed up at the end of Iron&nbsp;
Man and hinted at the idea of a full-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.080
shared universe of superhero movies, fans&nbsp;
were thrilled. And then The Incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.440
Hulk hit theaters a month later to prove you&nbsp;
should really be careful what you wish for.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.840
It's not that the film is terrible. But it's&nbsp;
something almost worse: forgettable. Which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.720
a real shame when you consider that it does so&nbsp;
much right. For one thing, if you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.400
make a movie about a nerdy scientist with anger&nbsp;
issues so powerful they could level a whole town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.120
getting the guy who starred in Fight Club&nbsp;
to play Bruce Banner is a pretty solid move.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.760
For another, the filmmakers realized&nbsp;
audiences didn't need a full-blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.640
origin story to get up to speed. Tying the&nbsp;
Hulk’s origin into Captain America and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.800
Super Soldier program was the first&nbsp;
stumbling step towards building the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.320
full-on shared universe. In the end,&nbsp;
though, it just didn't quite land.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:07.200
Critics praised director Chloe Zhao's "Eternals"&nbsp;
for its inclusivity and ambition, but story-wise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.720
it didn't live up to the hype.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.400
Unlike the vast majority of MCU&nbsp;
movies, "Eternals" is a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.640
too much like homework. With more&nbsp;
than a dozen new heroes and villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.720
the film feels somehow overstuffed and meandering&nbsp;
at the same time. Despite some big names in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.760
of and behind the camera, this cosmic epic&nbsp;
turned out undercooked. Discussions over whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.720
fault that is will likely continue for some&nbsp;
time; the general consensus in the Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.080
trades was that Oscar winner Zhao was hamstrung&nbsp;
by having to adhere to Marvel's strict formula.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.520
While it teases some exciting&nbsp;
future projects "Eternals"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.520
on its own is a convoluted mess that ranks&nbsp;
embarrassingly low in the MCU standings.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.240
"You are so pathetic!"

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.640
Sam Rockwell literally dancing across the stage to&nbsp;
present his army of Iron Man drones is endlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.160
delightful, but there's a lot in this movie that&nbsp;
doesn't involve Justin Hammer busting a move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.840
and that's where it fails to measure up. Hammer&nbsp;
and Whiplash both serve as evil versions of Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.800
Stark, bringing unnecessary complications to&nbsp;
the story. Even worse, the scene when Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.040
goes down into his basement with a particle&nbsp;
accelerator to somehow build a new element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:25.920
might be the goofiest thing in the entire MCU, and&nbsp;
those movies have a talking raccoon from space.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.680
At the same time, Iron Man 2&nbsp;
was a confident stride towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.440
the full MCU, introducing the&nbsp;
Black Widow and War Machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.160
and even teasing Thor's arrival in the&nbsp;
next film. There's a lot there to like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.880
and even if it's outweighed by the bad stuff,&nbsp;
we'll always have Justin Hammer's dance moves.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.360
Iron Man 2 might've been the first big step&nbsp;
towards fleshing out the Marvel Universe, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.760
Thor was the first time we actually&nbsp;
got to see its scope in action.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:00.560
With Thor we got Asgard in all its glory, complete&nbsp;
with a rainbow laser bridge, epic battles against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:08.560
frost giants, and—perhaps most importantly—those&nbsp;
big ol' Jack Kirby hats that Norse gods apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.440
love to wear. It even gave us a viking&nbsp;
god robot with a face made of death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:20.880
lasers. And it introduced arguably the&nbsp;
best villain in the whole MCU, Loki.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.920
Like Thor: The Dark World, though, the original&nbsp;
Thor film somehow ended up feeling like less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.280
than the sum of its parts. It should have&nbsp;
been great, but ended up merely... okay.

00:04:31.974 --> 00:04:34.800
Iron Man 3 tried to do something different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.560
and it succeeded brilliantly. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
what it was trying to do was defy expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.560
by completely undermining everything&nbsp;
fans loved about Iron Man and the MCU.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.600
Iron Man 3 explores how the smartest guy in the&nbsp;
room deals with living in a world that's becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.560
something that he can't predict. It's a movie&nbsp;
about a superhero with an anxiety disorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.440
which results in Iron Man spending the&nbsp;
entire movie not wanting to be Iron Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.440
and thus being Iron Man as little as possible.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.080
Plus, there was the whole fake Mandarin thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.800
which some people loved... but most fans&nbsp;
were either confused or disappointed by.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.760
Director Shane Black deserves&nbsp;
credit for his bold vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.520
which made for a good movie -&nbsp;
just not a good Marvel movie.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.720
When 2016's Doctor Strange was announced, the big&nbsp;
question for comics fans was how the mind-bending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.680
psychedelic sorcery of the mystical Marvel&nbsp;
Universe was going to fit into the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.880
As comics fans know, things get pretty weird.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.640
*psychedelic freakout*

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.960
And from a visual standpoint,&nbsp;
the filmmakers absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.240
nailed it. The splitting realities in the&nbsp;
movie's fight scenes were beautifully weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.480
and the ghostly ethereal plane where&nbsp;
life-and-death battles for the fate of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:56.640
Earth could rage unseen was great for showing&nbsp;
how different Stephen Strange's world was.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.080
From a story standpoint, though, it seemed a&nbsp;
little too familiar, as the story beats were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.360
basically Iron Man with magic. Still, you&nbsp;
can do worse than copying one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.760
popular superhero films ever, and&nbsp;
the fantastic climax, with Strange's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.880
time-bending bargain with Dormammu, is one&nbsp;
of the best finales in the MCU to date.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.440
Juggling a massive cast of superheroes is&nbsp;
a difficult task, and while Avengers: Age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:29.120
of Ultron doesn't manage it quite as well as the&nbsp;
other three Avengers films - or Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.560
Civil War - it does a pretty good job of balancing&nbsp;
character bits with all-out action. Sure, some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.920
parts seem half baked, like Tony Stark apparently&nbsp;
forgetting every lesson he learned in Iron Man 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.880
and Thor's weird spirit quest. But&nbsp;
it boasts a great final battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.120
not to mention the introduction of fan favorite&nbsp;
characters Vision and the Scarlet Witch.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.320
Avengers: Age of Ultron isn't perfect,&nbsp;
but it still packs in a lot of fun.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:02.160
All the way back in 2010, producer Kevin Feige&nbsp;
confirmed that a solo outing for Natasha Romanoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.160
was in the pipeline, but over a decade would pass&nbsp;
before "Black Widow" hit cineplexes and Disney+.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.760
Taking place right after the events of Captain&nbsp;
America: Civil War, Cate Shortland's prequel sends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.680
Natasha back to Budapest, to deal with her past.&nbsp;
There, she teams up with Yelena Belova, a fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.480
Red Room trainee who once posed as Romanoff's&nbsp;
sister, to take down the Black Widow program.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.800
Watching Romanoff right her wrongs after&nbsp;
so many years is undeniably satisfying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.320
and "Black Widow" benefits from&nbsp;
the fact that it largely stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.920
alone. It's a gripping watch and a&nbsp;
worthy sendoff for an OG Avenger.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.360
"This thing that you do when you&nbsp;
whip your hair when you're fighting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.800
With the arm and the hair, and&nbsp;
you do like a fighting pose."

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.320
The first Guardians of the Galaxy movie&nbsp;
proved that the MCU could get weird and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:56.320
cosmic and still be incredibly entertaining.&nbsp;
The second one proved that it wasn't a fluke.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.840
If anything, it went bigger. The first movie&nbsp;
brought us stuff like the Nova Corps and Ronan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.200
the Accuser, but Vol. 2 has about a half hour&nbsp;
of superheroes battling Ego the Living Planet.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.200
It's got waves of space drones&nbsp;
swarming into starship battles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.440
a warp drive sequence that knows exactly how&nbsp;
silly it is, and it features the best Stan Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:24.400
cameo in cinema history and the single&nbsp;
best use of a Zune in anything, ever.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.840
It's tempting to say Ant-Man shouldn't&nbsp;
have worked as well as it did, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.080
that a second-tier superhero with&nbsp;
the ability to get really tiny and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:40.400
talk to ants was a big surprise when it started to&nbsp;
rake in hundreds of millions of dollars. Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.200
though, it's not that unexpected. After&nbsp;
all, Marvel's first big movie success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.520
came from a pretty obscure D-Lister&nbsp;
from the pages of Tomb of Dracula.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.880
Audiences have always been interested in stories&nbsp;
that twist the expected superhero plot points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.160
around into something new, and that's where&nbsp;
Ant-Man really delivers. As easy as it would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:06.000
been to portray Scott Lang as a microscopic&nbsp;
version of Iron Man, his story felt different.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.400
"Hey, are we the good guys?"

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:08.880
"Yeah.”

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.760
"We’re the good guys, right?"

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.800
"Yeah, we’re the good guys."

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.120
"Feels kinda weird, ya know?"

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.960
The movie has its missteps, like going out of&nbsp;
its way to justify not having the Wasp show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.040
until the sequel, but on the other hand,&nbsp;
this is a movie in which the hero loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.480
a fight with a toy train, and Chekhov's&nbsp;
Gun is actually Chekhov's 60-Ton Tank.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.040
That The Avengers works at&nbsp;
all is pretty impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.200
but that it works as well as it&nbsp;
did? That's basically a miracle.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.000
Even less than a decade later, it's difficult&nbsp;
to remember that this was the first time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.360
anything like this had been attempted: a movie&nbsp;
that combined characters who had been established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:53.600
in their own films, each with their own tone and&nbsp;
style. In bringing them together, Joss Whedon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:10:00.560
had to balance the fantasy of Thor, the snarky&nbsp;
sci-fi of Iron Man, and the sincere superheroics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:06.480
of Captain America, combining them all—along&nbsp;
with Hulk, Hawkeye, and Black Widow—with stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:12.480
that were high enough to bring everyone together&nbsp;
for a single adventure. And this movie does it.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.400
Those big stakes mostly result from&nbsp;
an army of faceless aliens, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.440
even that paid off years later in Avengers:&nbsp;
Infinity War. And, Loki getting Hulk-Smashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.440
like a Looney Tunes character was the&nbsp;
most fun superhero movies had ever been.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.080
It's safe to say Iron Man surprised us all.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.240
It's not that we expected it to be bad.&nbsp;
Tony Stark's origin story is a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:38.720
good fit for a Hollywood action movie, it&nbsp;
had a great cast, and while director Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.920
Favreau might've been best known for Elf,&nbsp;
that really wasn’t a bad thing. But still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.680
none of us expected it to be so good that it&nbsp;
would become the foundation of the entire MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.560
To say that it was an exceptionally&nbsp;
powerful launch would be an understatement.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.400
There are times when this feels less&nbsp;
like a "superhero" movie and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.800
like a popcorn revenge flick where the hero&nbsp;
just happens to have a flying suit of armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.640
but it did its job so well that those&nbsp;
shortcomings are easy to forgive.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.960
Every set piece in Ant-Man and the Wasp&nbsp;
plays with the idea of changing size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.640
from the Wasp running along the blade of a knife&nbsp;
to Ant-Man using a flatbed truck as a scooter.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.440
Through it all, this sequel is genuinely&nbsp;
hilarious. After the relentless downer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.960
of "Avengers: Infinity War," this&nbsp;
lighthearted take on superheroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:31.760
with stakes far more personal than&nbsp;
cosmic, was a welcome tone shift.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.600
"What are you, laughing? Please, can you just…"

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:36.772
"Okay! Okay."

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.160
Arguably, the best thing about the MCU is&nbsp;
that it feels like a shared universe in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:46.880
same way that the comics do. The knowledge&nbsp;
that "Ant-Man and the Wasp" and "Infinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.000
War" happen at roughly the same time is maybe&nbsp;
the best expression of that. Thanos is solemnly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.160
searching for the Infinity Stones while Scott&nbsp;
Lang is running through an elementary school in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.680
a hoodie because he got stuck at 3 feet tall. And&nbsp;
it all sets up a heck of a gut punch at the end.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:07.840
The First Avenger does origin stories better&nbsp;
than most, and a lot of that has to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:14.400
Chris Evans. In a franchise that's full of amazing&nbsp;
casting choices, Evans pulls off the incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:20.720
feat of embodying a square-jawed, relentlessly&nbsp;
earnest product of the military-industrial complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.480
in a way that makes him impossible not to like.&nbsp;
For all that super-strength, there's a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.560
whose real super-power is making you believe in&nbsp;
him, and that’s exactly what this movie does.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:34.160
“I can do this all day.”

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:40.720
As good as it was on its own, it also serves as&nbsp;
the proof-of-concept for the superhero movie as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:46.960
a period piece, and gave the MCU a history that&nbsp;
went further back than 2008 and Tony Stark’s cave.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.640
The first Guardians of the Galaxy movie&nbsp;
manages to balance so much stuff going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.920
on that you almost don't notice how complicated&nbsp;
it is for all the fun you're having. It’s got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:02.560
team of lovable misfits dealing with their own&nbsp;
issues while fighting against a massive threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:07.120
expansive interstellar empires, and&nbsp;
a retro feel supported by a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.280
rock soundtrack that doesn't feel like&nbsp;
it's wallowing in the past. All that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.560
and still tells a story so fun that it&nbsp;
made Rocket Raccoon a household name.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:22.080
It breaks the formula in every way it can, to&nbsp;
the point where it's a superhero team movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:27.840
ends with a dance-off and the heroes literally&nbsp;
saving the universe with the power of friendship.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.240
"Far From Home" is built around the question&nbsp;
of what this universe looks like without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:40.560
its first superhero, Tony Stark. There's&nbsp;
a void in the MCU that needs to be filled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:46.800
and Spider-Man might just be the one to do&nbsp;
it. That's why Mysterio's plot goes all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:52.080
way back to the first "Iron Man" film and&nbsp;
weaves through the entire MCU timeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:58.080
Making it all make sense to cap off another&nbsp;
phase of the MCU was an amazing feat.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.884
All the good Spider-Man stuff is here: Peter&nbsp;
Parker has to wrestle with responsibility and self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.884 --> 00:14:10.240
doubt, the romance with MJ is heartfelt and sweet,&nbsp;
and there are serious consequences for Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:16.960
always walking around with his mask off. There are&nbsp;
world-shattering surprises and lots of fun special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.160
effects from a special-effects wizard villain.&nbsp;
It's a super fun time at the movie theater.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:27.520
There were some pretty heavy expectations&nbsp;
for Captain Marvel given that it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:32.400
MCU's first film led by a woman.&nbsp;
Luckily, it succeeded big time.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:37.200
Rather than falling into the tried and true Iron&nbsp;
Man formula that had been used to introduce so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:43.360
many other MCU characters, Captain Marvel was a&nbsp;
search for identity. That search took place over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:48.800
the backdrop of an intergalactic war, with the&nbsp;
title character's potential mostly represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:55.040
by the main character glowing with literal cosmic&nbsp;
fire while she punched her way through spaceships.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.480
At times, Captain Marvel may&nbsp;
come off as a little corny, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:05.280
that corniness is a result of simply being very&nbsp;
earnest and heartfelt, and at times inspiring.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.160
"My name…is Carol."

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:14.400
Between that, the cosmic action, and lots of&nbsp;
jokes about the '90s, it lived up to the hype.

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:21.200
Everything about The Winter Soldier reinforces&nbsp;
the idea that Captain America is exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:25.120
guy you want to have the power to&nbsp;
save the world. The notebook full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.360
of pop culture references that he needs to&nbsp;
look up to understand the world around him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:33.840
The friendship with Sam Wilson that starts&nbsp;
with bonding over their wartime experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.560
The loyalty to his friends that leads him&nbsp;
to risk his own life to save Bucky. He even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:44.240
gives his opponents one last chance to back out&nbsp;
before he beats up an elevator full of traitors.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:49.200
All of these pieces add up&nbsp;
to a whole that's incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.080
even before you get to how much of the&nbsp;
movie is based around Steve Rogers just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:58.080
kicking the living hell out of bad&nbsp;
guys. That stuff's pretty fun, too.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:03.440
Marvel's ultimate goal with Shang-Chi and&nbsp;
the Legend of the Ten Rings was to repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:07.920
the success of "Black Panther," according&nbsp;
to Deadline. And that plan largely worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:12.960
with critics agreeing it was a solid step&nbsp;
toward better Asian representation in Hollywood.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.840
Shang-Chi doesn't stray very far from Marvel's&nbsp;
tried-and-tested origin story formula, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:22.880
it sets itself apart when it comes to action.&nbsp;
Director Destin Daniel Cretton's movie pays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:27.840
homage to the fight choreography of Jackie&nbsp;
Chan, as well as Chinese martial arts films past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:32.960
and present. Simu Liu brings the perfect blend&nbsp;
of humility and heroism to the title role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:38.240
while Awkwafina, Tony Leung, and Michelle&nbsp;
Yeoh are all on their A-games too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:41.600
Add in a couple of unexpected&nbsp;
cameos, and you're onto a winner.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:45.520
"Wong! Wong! Wong! Wong! Wong! Wong! Wong!"

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:51.680
Even though Steve Rogers and Bucky Barnes&nbsp;
are unquestionably the focus of Civil War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:55.840
there's a pretty convincing argument to be&nbsp;
made that this movie should've been called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:57.760
Avengers: Civil War instead.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:03.520
It has everyone in it, and they aren't&nbsp;
just cameos, either. Ant-Man, Hawkeye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:08.000
Scarlet Witch and War Machine all get&nbsp;
huge, character-developing moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:12.240
in the middle of a superheroic Royal&nbsp;
Rumble, while the Avengers are split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:17.840
by a villain who wound up getting exactly&nbsp;
what he wanted. And if that wasn't enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:24.080
it's also the movie that introduced movie&nbsp;
audiences to Black Panther and Spider-Man.

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:28.400
The characters are never flattened out to&nbsp;
fit, and the three-way fight at the end has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:34.000
not only some of the best action in the MCU,&nbsp;
but some of the most emotional moments, too.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:37.120
After his debut in Civil War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:42.240
T’Challa’s first solo movie came&nbsp;
with some pretty high expectations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:49.920
Not only did it live up to them, it blew right&nbsp;
past them for one of the MCU’s best movies.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.760
Maybe it's because Chadwick Boseman's&nbsp;
T'Challa is a character struggling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:58.160
the weight of expectations himself. Black&nbsp;
Panther is more than up to the challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:03.200
but the journey he takes to get there&nbsp;
is full of compelling complications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:07.920
from the reveal that his father wasn’t the&nbsp;
flawlessly honorable man that he thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:12.960
to an enemy with some very justifiable&nbsp;
anger at the world around him. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:18.080
superhero struggle that goes far beyond&nbsp;
just the punch-out battle in the climax.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:22.880
Black Panther never shies away from&nbsp;
its high-tech comic book roots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:28.880
but tells a story that's very much rooted&nbsp;
in the real world, and does it beautifully.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:35.840
More than any other superhero movie,&nbsp;
Infinity War captures the feeling of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:42.080
an epic comic book crossover. There are multiple&nbsp;
interconnected stories going on at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:46.800
each with its own flavor, bridging&nbsp;
different story arcs and making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:51.520
every single character gets a moment in the&nbsp;
spotlight. When it comes together at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:57.360
it’s a fight across multiple planets where the&nbsp;
unbelievably high stakes become very real for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:03.120
both the characters and the fans who have been&nbsp;
invested in these movies for a full ten years.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:07.680
That’s a tough enough bit of storytelling&nbsp;
to pull off well even in comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:12.960
and they’ve had decades of practice. Seeing&nbsp;
it done here, in a single movie that still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:18.400
has the feeling of encompassing a whole&nbsp;
universe? No other movie has ever done that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.520
and it’s hard to imagine&nbsp;
another one doing it this well.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:27.280
As good as it is on its own, the fact&nbsp;
that Peter Parker swings into action after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:31.760
15 other movies means we don't have&nbsp;
to waste time with an origin story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:36.640
Thematically, Homecoming builds on everything&nbsp;
that came before in a truly incredible way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:41.440
with direct callbacks to Tony Stark's character&nbsp;
arc that show how different Peter is from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:46.080
hero he's trying to impress. The idea&nbsp;
of a superhero universe erupting around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:50.560
otherwise normal people is the conflict&nbsp;
that drives everything about the movie.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:54.160
Take that away and you've&nbsp;
still got great characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.160 --> 00:20:01.520
great action, and one of the best scenes in the&nbsp;
franchise. With it, though, you've got pure magic.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:08.240
To say that Ragnarok lifts heavily from Walt&nbsp;
Simonson's epic mid-'80s run of The Mighty Thor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:12.640
putting it mildly. The only things in this&nbsp;
movie that weren't directly inspired by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:18.000
Simonson are the pieces it pulls directly from&nbsp;
Marvel's more recent Planet Hulk storyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:23.520
right down to the Hulk's gladiator gear. The&nbsp;
thing is, Taika Waititi's film chops up all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:30.160
those pieces and rearranges them into something&nbsp;
that’s both different and genuinely hilarious.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:34.320
That part isn't surprising, considering&nbsp;
that before Ragnarok, Waititi was probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.760
best known for co-directing the vampire&nbsp;
mockumentary What We Do in the Shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.720
but it takes advantage of Chris Hemsworth's&nbsp;
infuriatingly good comedic timing.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:45.860
"Ow."

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:47.840
"Just had to be sure."

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:54.880
Three generations of Spider-Man movies collide&nbsp;
spectacularly in Spider-Man: No Way Home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:58.160
Tom Holland's Peter Parker is&nbsp;
once again the main focus, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:03.360
"No Way Home" is closer to an Avengers-style&nbsp;
team-up than the two films that precede it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:09.360
Doctor Strange plays a huge role, but some&nbsp;
characters from outside the MCU show up, too.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:14.320
In what was one of the worst-kept&nbsp;
secrets in MCU history, Tobey Maguire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:19.600
and Andrew Garfield appear to offer Holland's&nbsp;
Peter Parker sage advice and much-needed comfort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:24.480
in the fight to save the lives of the villains&nbsp;
from those two actors' own Spider-Man flicks.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:29.280
Holland finally gets his "With great&nbsp;
power comes great responsibility" moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:36.160
and it's one that you won't forget. The Brit turns&nbsp;
in his most accomplished performance as Spidey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:43.107
backed up by the all-star supporting cast. No&nbsp;
Way Home is the best Spider-Man movie to date.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:48.800
Endgame could have gone wrong really&nbsp;
easily. It's meant to serve as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:52.960
climax of the 21 movies before&nbsp;
it in the first saga of the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:57.840
so saying that Marvel pulled it off is saying&nbsp;
that they pretty much achieved a miracle.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:02.320
The film is loaded with callbacks&nbsp;
in ways that you don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:06.400
realize until you stop to think about&nbsp;
them: Captain America's support group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:13.200
New Asgard, Black Widow wiping out the red in her&nbsp;
ledger, and the list goes on. It's payoff after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:19.040
payoff, but it never feels like pure fan&nbsp;
service. It really does feel like an ending.

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:25.040
Plus, Endgame has plenty of great moments&nbsp;
on its own. The time-travel hijinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:30.720
the debut of Professor Hulk, the audacity&nbsp;
of skipping ahead five years, and the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:38.000
fist-pumping awesomeness of Cap tightening up his&nbsp;
shield and standing alone against the apocalypse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:43.200
It really is the thundering climax&nbsp;
fans have wanted from the start.

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:44.640
"Is that everyone?"

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:46.400
"What, you wanted more?"

